Bad pixels: interpolate the map sequentially.

Bad pixels are interpolated in place inside an OpenMP parallel loop. When
two bad pixels are neighbours, one reads the other before or after it is
repaired depending on thread timing: the result changes with the thread
count (measured on a real CRAW frame: single-threaded output equals the
host build, 8 threads differ). The map holds ~10^3 pixels, so the
sequential loop is cheap and gives MLV-App's single-threaded result.

--- a/src/mlv/llrawproc/pixelproc.c
+++ b/src/mlv/llrawproc/pixelproc.c
@@ -1466,1 +1466,1 @@
-            #pragma omp parallel for
+            /* sequential: in-place interpolation of neighbouring bad pixels is order dependent */
